    "463": {
      "date": "2026-11-03",
      "edition": "Tuesday 3 November",
      "sections": [
        {
          "kicker": "Tactical analysis",
          "heading": "Japan's new kick-catch switch now being countered by opponents, note.com analysis says",
          "body": "The analysis describes a shift Japan have made this year: on high kicks player B, not player A, takes the catch, which cuts the risk of conceding penalties for escorting but leaves the catcher running backwards with only one team-mate in support. The writer says the method is now being countered, with pressure on the catch or a deeper kick among the options, and that the issue was highlighted in the Fiji match on 24 October, which Japan lost 28-34."
